/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - For a stagnant film, molecular diffusion gave NA = DAB(CA1 − CA2)/δ, where DAB is diffusivity (m²/s) and δ is film thickness (m).
   - NA is the molar flux of A across the interface, mol/(m²·s); CAi is the concentration of A just at the interface, mol/m³; CA∞ is the concentration of A in the well-mixed bulk fluid, mol/m³; kc is the convective mass transfer coefficient, m/s
   - The unit follows: mol/(m²·s) divided by mol/m³ gives m/s.
   - All the unknown fluid mechanics — film thickness, turbulence, surface shape — is pushed inside kc.
   - For a gas, partial pressure is the natural driving force.
   - The coefficients come in two types, because the flux formula for molecular diffusion differs with what B does.
   - A local coefficient applies at one point and varies with position because the boundary layer changes.
   - Air flows over a flat plate.
   - The layer thickens roughly as √x from the leading edge.
   - In turbulent flow, eddies add an eddy diffusivity far larger than the molecular one, so bulk transport is fast.
*/
