/* book exam traps: For 1/Ky the liquid term is m/kx. For 1/Kx the gas term is 1/(m ky). Reversing m is the commonest error. | Do not add 1/ky and 1/kx directly. They sit on different driving forces. Convert with m first. | Ky and Kx have different numerical values and different driving-force bases. A resistance fraction must use the same basis as the coefficient. | m is the slope of y* = m x. If the equilibrium line is curved, use the chord slopes m′ (for Ky) and m″ (for Kx); a single m is valid only for a straight line. | The interface is in equilibrium, not the bulk phases. y* = m x uses the bulk liquid x, not the interface xi. */
